.TH VTED 1 "November 2026" "vted 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vted \- vex terminal text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vted
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.RI [ +LINE ]
.RI [ file | \- ] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vted
is a small, fast terminal text editor for Linux written in C11 with no
dependencies beyond the C standard library and POSIX.
.PP
It puts the terminal into raw mode, switches to the alternate screen
buffer and restores every terminal setting on exit. Every
.I file
named on the command line opens in a buffer of its own and the
editor starts in the first one; with no file it starts with an empty
buffer. Anything that looks like an option is rejected on the standard
error stream before the terminal is touched.
.PP
Several files can be edited in one session: each buffer keeps its
cursor, viewport and undo history while another one is being edited,
the status bar says which of them is on ("2/3, Ln 5/120, Col 12"), and
opening a path that is already open switches to that buffer instead
of opening it twice. Ctrl\-B lists the open buffers over the bottom of
the viewport with their numbers and names (a + marks unsaved changes):
the arrows wrap around the list, Enter takes the one under the
selection, Ctrl\-E closes it without leaving the list (unsaved
changes ask for three more presses first) and ESC stays on the
buffer that was live. Ctrl\-E closes
the buffer being edited; unsaved changes require three additional
confirmations first, and closing the last buffer starts a fresh empty
one. Unsaved changes in any buffer hold a quit back.
.PP
The whole interface is drawn on the standard output, so
.B vted
refuses to start when that is not a terminal. Keys are read from the
terminal, which may be the controlling one when standard input holds
piped text instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
print a short usage on the standard output and exit
.TP
.BR \-V , " " \-\-version
print the version and exit
.TP
.BI + LINE
start the cursor on that line of the first file, the way less and vi
take one; a number past the end of the buffer lands on the last line
.TP
.B \-
read the text from standard input instead of a file. The buffer stays
unnamed, so Ctrl\-S asks where to save it, and it can sit beside
files ("vted \- notes.txt"). Standard input must not be a terminal:
nothing would ever arrive on it, and it can be named only once
.PP
Every line is numbered in a gutter at the left edge, the line the
cursor is on in bold. The gutter is at least three digits wide and
grows with the file; a terminal too narrow to leave room for the text
beside it drops the numbers. A line that wraps over several screen
lines is numbered on its first one; the lines under it line up in
blanks.
.PP
The text is colored by what it means: comments, strings, numbers,
keywords, types and preprocessor lines, and the added and removed
lines of a diff. The language is picked from
the file name; C, C++, Python, shell scripts, Makefiles, JavaScript,
TypeScript, Rust, Go, JSON, Markdown, HTML, XML, CSS, Lua, Ruby, YAML,
TOML, INI, SQL, Dockerfiles and diffs are
known, and any other name is drawn plain. The colors are the
terminal's own, so they follow its
theme; COLORTERM=truecolor|24bit asks for RGB escapes, a 256-color
terminal gets 256-color escapes, anything else uses the 16 colors.
.PP
The bottom line of the screen is a status bar showing the file name,
line count, a modified marker and the cursor's line and display
column ("Ln 5/120, Col 12"; with several buffers open, which one this
is comes first, as "2/3, Ln 5/120, Col 12"; narrow terminals show the
line alone, then nothing). The line above it is a message bar for
status messages and warnings.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B arrows
move the cursor, wrapping across line ends
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Left / Ctrl\-Right
move by word; Alt\-Left, Alt\-Right, Alt\-b and Alt\-f do the same
.TP
.B Home / End
start / end of the line, or of the wrapped screen line the cursor
sits on
.TP
.B PageUp / PageDown
move one viewport up / down, of screen lines when wrapping is on
.TP
.B Enter
split the line at the cursor; the new line starts with the indent of
the current one
.TP
.B Tab
insert a tab character, shown up to the next tab stop (every 8 cells)
.TP
.B Backspace
delete the character to the left, merge lines at column zero
.TP
.B Delete
delete the character under the cursor
.TP
.B Ctrl\-K
delete the whole line under the cursor; deleting the only line
empties it
.TP
.B Ctrl\-D
duplicate the line under the cursor and move onto the copy
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Up / Ctrl\-Down
move the current line up or down; the cursor rides the line
.TP
.B Shift\-Arrows
start or extend a character selection
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C / Ctrl\-X
copy or cut the selection (or the current line) to the system clipboard
via an OSC 52 escape sequence
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
search and replace every occurrence of a string; each replacement is one
undo step
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
undo the last change and put the cursor back where it was; a run of
typing or backspace keystrokes in one place counts as a single
change, so one press takes back the whole burst; the last 200
changes are kept
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Y
redo the change that Ctrl\-Z undid
.TP
plain typing
insert at the cursor (UTF\-8 aware)
.TP
.B Ctrl\-S
save the buffer to its file; a buffer without a name asks for one first,
ESC cancels
.TP
.B Ctrl\-O
open another file as a buffer of its own and switch to it; a path that
does not exist yet opens as an empty buffer that keeps the name, and a
path that is already open switches to that buffer instead
.TP
.B Ctrl\-E
close the buffer being edited; the neighbor comes into view, and
closing the last buffer starts a fresh empty one. Unsaved changes
require three additional confirmations first
.TP
.B Ctrl\-B
list the open buffers over the bottom of the viewport; the arrows
move through the list (wrapping around both ends), Enter takes the
one under the selection, Alt\-1 to Alt\-9 take theirs straight away
and ESC stays on the buffer that was live
.TP
.B Alt\-, / Alt\-.
step through the open buffers, wrapping around both ends
.TP
.B Alt\-1 to Alt\-9
jump to the buffer with that number
.TP
.B Ctrl\-G
go to a line number; a number past the end goes to the last line, and
the line lands mid-viewport with wrapping's tall rows counted, ESC
cancels
.TP
.B Ctrl\-F
incremental search: arrows jump between matches, Enter keeps the
cursor on the match, ESC restores the previous position
.TP
.B Ctrl\-N
show or hide the line numbers
.TP
.B Ctrl\-L
wrap long lines over several screen lines instead of scrolling the
viewport sideways; the up and down arrows then step over the wrapped
lines one at a time, PageUp and PageDown move a viewport of them, and
a line taller than the viewport scrolls inside itself
.TP
.B Ctrl\-T
toggle Tab between inserting spaces up to the next tab stop and
inserting a tab character
.TP
.B Ctrl\-W
set the tab width in cells (1 to 32); the buffer being edited keeps
the width as its own, and a buffer opened later starts with the width
the live one uses
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Q
quit; unsaved changes in any of the open buffers require three
additional confirmations
.SH TEXT HANDLING
The cursor moves and deletes by whole UTF\-8 characters, never into
the middle of a multibyte sequence. Wide characters (CJK, emoji) take
two terminal cells and combining marks none. Control characters are
shown as
.BI ^ X
in inverted video and bytes that are not valid UTF\-8 as U+FFFD.
When moving up and down the cursor aims for the display column it
started from, even across shorter lines.
.PP
Moving by words treats a run of letters, digits and underscores (in any
script) as one word and a run of punctuation as another; blanks
separate them. At the end or the start of a line a word move continues
on the neighbouring line.
.PP
The bracket the cursor sits on, or the one right behind it, has its
partner drawn in inverted video: the scan counts brackets of the same
pair, so nesting comes out right, and it reaches a megabyte before a
match counts as missing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B vted
exits with status 0 after a normal quit, and with status 0 again for
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
A rejected command line, a terminal that is not there (standard output
not a terminal, "\-" on a terminal standard input) and fatal errors (a
file that cannot be opened, terminal setup failures) terminate the
editor with an error message on the standard error stream and a
non\-zero status.
.SH FILES
The buffers are saved back to the
.I files
given on the command line. Without one, Ctrl\-S asks for a file name;
the buffer takes that name only if the file could be written, and ESC
cancels the save.
.PP
The settings live in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/vted/vtedrc
(or
.IR ~/.config/vted/vtedrc
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset). Each line names one setting with
.B "set tab-width"
.I N
(1 to 32),
.B "set insert-spaces on"
or
.BR off ,
and
.B "set line-numbers on"
or
.BR off ,
and
.B "set wrap on"
or
.BR off .
Blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped, and a later line
wins over an earlier one. A line that cannot be used is reported in
the message bar while the setting it would have set stays at its
default. The keys match what Ctrl\-W, Ctrl\-T, Ctrl\-N and Ctrl\-L set
while editing.
.PP
Line endings are kept as found: LF, CRLF, CR and mixed files save
back unchanged, byte for byte, and a file without a trailing newline
keeps having none. Lines created while editing take the ending of the
line they were made from.
.PP
Saving is atomic: the text is written to a temporary file next to the
target and renamed over it, so a save that fails leaves the original
untouched. The file's permissions are kept; a newly created file gets
the usual ones.
